\begin{abstract}
Continuous-state language models (flow-matching models that denoise a sequence of token embeddings) are differentiable end to end, yet the reinforcement-learning recipes being ported to them are the score-function estimators of autoregressive RLHF. We argue, and then measure, that this is the wrong estimator for this architecture: an autoregressive policy makes $\sim$30 low-dimensional semantic decisions per trajectory, whereas the ``action'' of a flow language model is a path of $10^{5}$--$10^{6}$ Gaussian noise coordinates, none of which individually decides a token. Every score-function variant we tried (oracle-exact advantages, per-step per-position credit, clipped per-position ratios, critic-selected advantages) caps at $+3$--$+5$ points on Countdown while the same estimator lifts a GPT-2 baseline by $+9.7$. We propose \jpc{} (joint pathwise critic): a \emph{pathwise} policy gradient through a \hl{jointly trained per-token (2D) critic} that scores every position of a noisy intermediate state, trained on the same rollouts as the policy under the deployment sampler, with a single random denoising step per update. The 2D critic is the object of the method: its pooled value is the pathwise objective, and its per-position outputs are a by-product used for measurement and filtering; a scalar critic is its ``pool-then-head'' special case. On four verifiable tasks (Countdown, Sudoku, Knights-and-Knaves, and a set-split Game24 that tests generalisation to unseen number sets) \jpc{} with the 2D critic is the only recipe that is stable and replicable across seeds, reaching $+13.7$ points on Game24 at the deployment sampler ($3$ seeds, $\sigma\,1.9$) where GRPO gives $+2.1$ and an autoregressive GPT-2 with token-level PPO gives $+5.3$. Finally, amortising the RL policy's own verified samples into the model by supervised fine-tuning lifts cheap samplers by up to $+17$ points and the deployment sampler further; two controls show that these gains require the RL policy (the identical loop from the SFT base gives $+2$, and gold derivations for the same problems give $0$).
\end{abstract}
